---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const siteUrl = SITE.url.replace(/\/$/, '');

const privacyLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Privacy: nothing leaves your browser',
  url: `${siteUrl}/privacy/`,
  description:
    'CertKit privacy: files are processed locally in your browser, nothing is uploaded, no accounts, anonymous visit counts only. AdSense is planned, not live yet.',
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'LocalCertKit',
    url: `${siteUrl}/`,
  },
};
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: nothing leaves your browser"
  description="CertKit privacy: files are processed locally in your browser, nothing is uploaded, no accounts, anonymous visit counts only. AdSense is planned, not live yet."
  jsonLd={[privacyLd]}
>
  <div class="wrap tool-wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Privacy</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <header class="tool-head">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lead">The short version: your files never leave your browser, and there are no accounts, no profiles and no uploads. The one thing that will change is advertising: Google AdSense is planned for some pages, and the rules for it are set out below.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>What happens to your files</h2>
      <p>
        Every tool on this site reads your file with the browser’s File API and processes it with
        JavaScript that is served from this domain. There is no upload step, no API call and no
        server-side processing. When you close the tab, the file is gone from memory.
      </p>
      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No file, key, password or certificate is transmitted anywhere.</li>
        <li>No first-party cookies, no localStorage, no fingerprinting, no session recording.</li>
        <li>
          No ad code today and no tracking pixels, no social embeds. Visit counts are anonymous and
          cookie-less. Read the <a href="#visit-counts">Visit counts</a> and{' '}
          <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a> sections below.
        </li>
        <li>No accounts, no email collection, no newsletter.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is static and hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Like virtually every web host, Cloudflare
        receives standard request metadata (IP address, user agent, requested URL) when your browser
        loads a page. That is how the HTML reaches you. No request that runs a tool carries your
        data, so that metadata never includes a file, key or password. See{' '}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>
        for how they handle request logs.
      </p>
      <h2 id="visit-counts">Visit counts</h2>
      <p>
        The site loads one small third-party script: Cloudflare Web Analytics
        (<span class="mono">static.cloudflareinsights.com</span>). Until ads are approved, it is the
        only request the page makes to a host other than this one, and it does one thing: records
        that a page was opened. It sets no cookies, stores nothing on your device, does not
        fingerprint you and does not follow you across other sites. The aggregate report shows which
        page was viewed, the referring site, the country and the browser family; never an individual
        visitor, and never anything about the files you open in a tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a visit counter, not surveillance. Cloudflare processes these aggregate counts under
        its own privacy policy (linked above). The tools themselves still make no network calls at
        all.
      </p>
      <h2 id="advertising">Advertising (AdSense)</h2>
      <p>
        No ads appear on CertKit today. We are applying to show Google AdSense ads on some pages.
        They will only appear after Google approves the site, and only with consent where required.
        Ad scripts can set cookies after consent; the tools themselves still run locally and never
        upload your files.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>After approval only.</strong> No AdSense script is loaded until Google approves the
          site. There is no ad slot, no placeholder and no hidden ad code on any page right now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consent first where required.</strong> In regions that require it, ad and consent
          scripts load only after you agree. The tools do not depend on ad scripts, so blocking or
          declining ads does not disable them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cookies and third-party data.</strong> After consent, Google and its partners may
          set cookies or read device identifiers to serve and measure ads. That can include
          approximate location and ad-interaction data. It is Google’s processing, not ours, and it
          never touches the files you open in a tool. See{' '}
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads" rel="noopener">Google’s advertising policies</a>
          and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google’s privacy policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The tools stay local.</strong> Ads sit beside the tools. Certificate, key, CSR and
          password contents never go to Google or anyone else: processing still happens in your
          browser tab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Opting out.</strong> You can limit ad personalisation at{' '}
          <a href="https://adssettings.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Ad Settings</a> or with your
          browser’s tracking controls. Blocking ads does not break any tool.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the application is rejected, or this changes, this section will be updated before anything
        ships.
      </p>
      <h2>Verifying the claims</h2>
      <p>
        Open DevTools → Network, clear the log, then run any tool: you will see no request appear
        while your file is processed. The one exception is the “Load sample” button, which fetches
        that test file from this site (again, nothing of yours is involved). The only third-party
        entry today is the analytics beacon loaded with the page; once AdSense is approved and
        consented to, Google’s ad script will appear there too. Disconnect your network after a page
        loads and the tools keep working. That is the strongest possible proof that processing is
        local.
      </p>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about the privacy model? Write to{' '}
        <a href="mailto:contact@wheelchairrampcalculator.com">contact@wheelchairrampcalculator.com</a>{' '}
        or see the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. The parsing library, tests and site source
        are public at{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/pantha704/certkit-mini-saas">github.com/pantha704/certkit-mini-saas</a>,
        open an issue if anything looks wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">Last updated: 20 October 2026.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
